#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163058} :activity/id #uuid "98dc20a6-b50c-5324-b83b-6ac8d56ecb70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048491 :topic/id #uuid "a4553111-5fc5-5a4e-8071-e4805050c5b5" :topic/math-academy-id 3069} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163322 :tutorial/content "An important family of distributions in probability and statistics is the **student's $t$-distribution** (or $t$-**distribution**). The diagram below shows the probability density functions for some members of this family.\n\n![](images/08/08b2d66fda68155c6440568508dde075812a7fe0e7a855e1efe175df52830719.png)\n\nEach family member has an associated positive integer parameter $k,$ known as the **degrees of freedom**. \n\nThe probability density function of a student's $t$-distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped, similar to the standard normal distribution. However, $t$-distribution curves have fatter \"tails.\" A consequence is that extreme events (i.e., events far from the mean) are more likely with a $t$-distribution than a standard normal distribution.\n\nTo see how the student's $t$-distribution comes about, consider a large random sample of size $n$\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\ndrawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2.$ According to the central limit theorem, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean. \n\nIn reality, it's unlikely that the population variance $\\sigma^2$ is known, in which case it must be estimated. \n\nRecall that an unbiased estimator for $\\sigma^2,$ known as the sample variance, is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIf we estimate $\\sigma^2$ using $S^2,$ then it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a $t$-distributed random variable with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nThe $t$-distribution is widely used in statistical estimation problems. We'll learn more about the practical applications of this important distribution in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "86069b59-4cfe-5965-9f08-bd7cbfca0b5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6250 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9e43698a-bcd5-5e90-92a2-a01cfff08635" :step/math-academy-id 37900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163323 :tutorial/content "When working with $t$-distributed random variables, we often want to find a value that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, let $X\\sim T_{21}$ be a $t$-distributed random variable with $21$ degrees of freedom. Suppose we want to find the value of $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.01.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we wish to find the $x$-value corresponding to the shaded area shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c3ef041152494232ecb60408580965018132a024c406dcde0506ff519d7ed73.png)\n\nTo compute these probabilities, we use a **percentage points table**. A typical percentage points table for the student's $t$-distribution is shown below.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nThe rows correspond to different degrees of freedom $k.$ Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = p\n$$\n\nfor a particular value of $p.$\n\nSince $X \\sim T_{21},$ we focus on the table row corresponding to $k=21.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2.518}$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}2.518}) = {\\color{blue}0.01}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 2.518,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d90b9aec3439d4425f9d8998d7795576f5eb714f5bee93784cd556c18b721bd7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "20595d2d-9126-5da7-a3b4-8b66e140c084" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6251 :tutorial/title "Working With a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "3bfbeda8-b950-5e16-9bf2-bf3182e393e6" :step/math-academy-id 37901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161666 :question/id #uuid "7898bd86-2b83-5cdd-af38-43e9ac9004e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10042"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7e6407-bb34-5603-b325-580ac32f8a9d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500350}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a T-Score"} :step/id #uuid "ba42cc6b-6af3-5749-a32a-68baeb82fed6" :step/math-academy-id 37902 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161667 :question/id #uuid "86f614ee-dea5-5083-b18b-711f3e2bd84f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10043"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f2479b-d8f2-5042-a571-7ba4c713dad8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514465}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "c66b3235-0142-5eb2-ac43-5b3dfd36c9e5" :step/math-academy-id 37903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163324 :tutorial/content "The shape of the student's $t$-distribution is similar to that of the standard normal distribution. Are they related, and if so, how?\n\nThe two distributions are indeed related. In fact, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1)$ as $k \\to \\infty.$*\n\nThe diagram below shows the student's $t$-distribution for different degrees of freedom $k$ and the standard normal distribution $N(0,1).$ \n\n![](images/94/942080761ebe53527c36102c5e037eb9ee6ef7cb896aca7df130a4992fc6b0d6.png)\n\nNow, as $k\\to\\infty,$ we have that\n\n- the tails of $T_k$ become slimmer,\n\n- the peak of $T_k$ (i.e., the mode) becomes taller, and\n\n- $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d267cc0-5208-54de-b750-e18e7728902a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6252 :tutorial/title "Comparing the T-Distribution With the Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "514eead9-fd3a-559f-a1d9-63cf6c58b5a1" :step/math-academy-id 37906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161669 :question/id #uuid "1f694b6c-8ece-5e51-9618-5158e23521fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-10045"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6665557-2c04-5082-ba56-28f9376f00e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514439}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "ea119e88-4615-5679-b368-a44c2428c2ef" :step/math-academy-id 37909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163062}}] :activity/title "The Student's T-Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162198}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514526} :activity/id #uuid "627cdea7-e74f-570d-9499-4dfeee52d335" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048491 :topic/id #uuid "a4553111-5fc5-5a4e-8071-e4805050c5b5" :topic/math-academy-id 3069} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1faf8fac-95d9-5c9b-afb5-041607bd6022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.602" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513616} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0d5475-2c71-5b00-9cf3-9aedb6ecd6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.341" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513744} {:answer/id #uuid "59e0ee7f-17c9-57f5-a020-609a22fffdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513773} {:answer/id #uuid "7d78c7b2-e987-5f4d-ba6c-f50a26e8560e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513859} {:answer/id #uuid "88cbab98-0bfd-5b51-8738-97197bbd173d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.567" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513616} :answer-field/id #uuid "faac0f2c-9586-5270-b3d8-5b3c6c767d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3619ef06-3622-5375-8459-4e89f07dd7d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-142722" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{15},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.02.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.02$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.02\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.01.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{15},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=15.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.567$ | $2.898$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.602)=0.010.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.602.$"} :step/id #uuid "3021eda6-e3b8-5219-817c-73b31f6cb117" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5a030c-04ff-5537-a509-3d205630e231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.779" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513692} {:answer/id #uuid "42153ec8-896b-5a88-a532-420303c9dfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.708" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513708} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e04a1f-348f-5bb3-a5be-0930d2f13a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.318" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513953} {:answer/id #uuid "ec242ef5-dc63-533d-8db7-e947ec25bdd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.787" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514151} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc85b2f-6e5e-5e04-b574-f8f024c0b55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514151} :answer-field/id #uuid "885ef5b5-44d7-500b-bb70-819519766275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53bedfc9-6dd3-5f58-8bd9-2e76db557744" :question/math-academy-id "q-142580" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{25},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.995.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.995 \\\\  & =0.005\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{25},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=25.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.787)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.787.$"} :step/id #uuid "54c28814-14fa-5e14-be7b-d99db191689b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514496}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dbcaed-89eb-5fb0-b72c-f9dd1752a026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.708" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513534} {:answer/id #uuid "5b781a62-5301-58de-9505-2d037b1f3b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.479" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513776} {:answer/id #uuid "610f6000-ba93-5b31-be25-ad092c76ffb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.787" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513794} {:answer/id #uuid "71dcdc44-7b64-5426-8a7d-e4cd03d1e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513835} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a7bb1-9caf-57b8-b091-7437f18b4f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.797" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b63d849-ac8e-58fe-8d8d-780397c8a283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9663f7f5-26ed-525b-a4e7-8ebcf7b4896a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142729" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{25},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.01\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.005.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{25},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=25.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.787)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.787.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c114556-2f90-598a-a61e-2e9035fcc22d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2356bf-311c-51bb-9c39-b10fe214fff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513646} {:answer/id #uuid "527bfb4f-9d52-595b-b3f0-ce4b1acefcef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513760} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7993b3-f733-5e81-92fe-d298f6220041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513856} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf90eb5-a13e-5686-9760-9e87d8d3d500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.539" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513896} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3bcfd-2937-5d70-b9ad-bb649cdc22c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.861" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513856} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aecb7f6-bd05-5c5c-981f-c41f2b10cce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5be6574-3a28-52d0-8e4b-f267e2db636b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142541" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{19},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.9 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{19},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=19.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 1.328)=0.100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.328.$"} :step/id #uuid "b88b88a1-0ae2-5a32-886d-78f1dc75d173" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317af612-bd74-5d49-94a6-9a8a6e10174d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513663} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3a2f76-a9c8-57ad-af4f-54fc0e6cd390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513697} {:answer/id #uuid "82d3ce4e-9325-578a-bfa5-1a9cb4acdcf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513874} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a2c2e4-f556-523a-a75e-2e4591426514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513955} {:answer/id #uuid "c000eb5b-de3d-5c48-8197-770d70f4bc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514039} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb69b1a1-571c-5439-bbf9-3a6f22a0ac17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c576b08-9f07-530e-b539-76adf5c7de7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142543" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{20},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.99.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.99 \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{20},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=20.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.528)=0.010.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.528.$"} :step/id #uuid "d0868bbc-0e0e-57f2-be63-3eb0673252c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514503}}] :activity/title "The Student's T-Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513515}]}
